/* ============================================================================
monte-carlo-pi.epher -- estimating pi by throwing points at a circle

Throw n points uniformly into the unit square and count how many land
inside the quarter circle x^2 + y^2 <= 1. The square has area 1 and
the quarter circle has area pi/4, so the count ratio estimates pi/4
and 4 * hits / n estimates pi. With 10,000 points the standard error
is about 0.02, so the estimate should sit within a couple of percent
of pi; the seeded run below lands well inside that band.

The draws come from random() after randseed(7), and the random stream
is reproducible: the same seed replays the same points, so every run
of this file prints the same estimate.

Source: the classic Monte Carlo method; see Ross, "Simulation",
chapter 4.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/probability/simulations/monte-carlo-pi.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\probability\simulations\monte-carlo-pi.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/probability/simulations/monte-carlo-pi.epher
Web/desktop:             paste the file into the entry, press Enter

The knob to edit is `points`; everything below it is machinery.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knobs ----
const points = 10000

/* ---- results ----
The seeded thrower: one random x and one random y per point, both in
[0, 1), counted when x^2 + y^2 <= 1. The counting loop lives in a
definition with a do-body (guide 1.11): the for walks the known number
of points, the if behind it does the counting, and the block hands
back the hits. */
def thrower(n) do
  hits = 0
  for i in 1 to n do if random() ^ 2 + random() ^ 2 <= 1 then hits = hits + 1
  hits
end
randseed(7)
hits = thrower(points)

// The estimate and the comparison with the true pi.
print("hits inside the quarter circle:", hits)
print("estimate of pi:", 4 * hits / points)
print("true pi:", pi)
print("relative error:", (4 * hits / points - pi) / pi)
/* ---- expected output ----
= 10000
= 7
= 7895
= hits inside the quarter circle: 7895
= estimate of pi: 3.158
= true pi: 3.14159265359
= relative error: 0.00522262056841
*/
